{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Draw near, you, and hear… and you shall speak to us.</b> What did they have in mind? If it is possible for Moses to hear the voice of HaShem and not die — then it is possible for them as well; and if it is withheld from them — then it is withheld from Moses as well. And the expression ״וְאַתְּ״, as one speaking to a female, requires explanation, for what Rashi explained — 'your strength has been weakened like a female,' etc. — is insufficient, since HaShem said: ״הֵטִיבוּ כָּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּרוּ״ ('they have done well in all that they have spoken'). And furthermore, why did He say: ״קְרַב אַתָּה וּשְׁמָע״ — why is ״אַתָּה״ used, as one speaking to a male? And there it is written: ״אֲשֶׁר יֹאמַר״ — that is, soft speech (אֲמִירָה); and afterward it is written: ״וְאַתְּ תְּדַבֵּר״ — hard speech (דִּבּוּר).</p><p><b>And the explanation for all of this is</b> that as long as the strength of the material is weakened for a person, the intellectual power grows greater, and the wisdom of the aged will testify. Therefore Moses was able to hear the voice of HaShem and was not affected by it, for his material strength had already been weakened like a female through his separation from his wife and other practices of abstinence that were upon him, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Yoma 4b): ״וַיְכַסֵּהוּ הֶעָנָן שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״ (Exodus 24:16) — to purge the food and drink in his bowels, according to the one who says that the cloud covered Moses. And all this weakening of material power causes the strengthening of the intellect, which is not affected by the voice, for nothing is affected except by its opposite. And he was able to receive the voice of HaShem even hard speech (דִּבּוּר קָשֶׁה). Therefore they said: ״קְרַב אַתָּה וּשְׁמָע אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר יֹאמַר״ — for the soft speech (אֲמִירָה רַכָּה) of the Shechinah you are able to receive even if your material were strong as a male and had not been weakened like a female. But ״וְאַתְּ תְּדַבֵּר אֵלֵינוּ אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר יְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ אֵלֶיךָ״ — for you are able to receive even hard speech (דִּבּוּר קָשֶׁה), since your material has already been weakened like a female, for the face of Moses was like a illuminating lens that shines from every side, so the material within him was purified as well. And concerning this it was well said: ״הֵטִיבוּ כָּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּרוּ״.</p><p><b>And according to Rashi's explanation, the interpretation will be</b> that Moses was pained that they did not serve from love, for every lover clings to his beloved, while one who fears distances himself from Him. And Moses was pained by what they said: ״קְרַב אַתָּה וּשְׁמָע״, for the one who distances himself indicates fear, and after this Moses's strength was weakened on account of pain; therefore it is written: ״וְאַתְּ תְּדַבֵּר״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״הֵטִיבוּ כָּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּרוּ״ — as a gesture of appeasement, for according to the hardness of their stubbornness, it is a novelty to Me even this much — that they grasped hold of the trait of fear; and even from fear I am concerned lest they not endure in it for many days, as it says: ״מִי יִתֵּן וְהָיָה לְבָבָם זֶה לָהֶם לְיִרְאָה אוֹתִי… כָּל הַיָּמִים״ etc.</p><p><b>And there are those who interpret the verses in a homiletical vein,</b> that Israel said: since the benefit of death is only to remove from a person the material that is an interposing barrier between him and his Elohim, so that a person might be able to see the face of the Shechinah — for a person shall not see Him and live — and now we see that Elohim speaks with a person and he lives, ״וְעַתָּה לָמָּה נָמוּת״ — why should we suffer the pain of death for nothing? ״כִּי תֹאכְלֵנוּ הָאֵשׁ הַגְּדוֹלָה״ — that is, the suffering of death. Nevertheless, ״אִם יוֹסְפִים אֲנַחְנוּ״ to receive some greater perfection beyond what we would have in life regarding the hearing of the voice of HaShem, then ״וָמָתְנוּ״ — we shall accept upon ourselves the suffering of death."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "Rashi's comment, which the Kli Yakar cites as 'הִתַּשְׁתֶּם כֹּחִי כִּנְקֵבָה' ('your strength has been weakened like a female'), corresponds to Rashi on Deuteronomy 5:24 s.v. ואת תדבר. The phrase 'וְדַעַת זְקֵנִים יוֹכִיחַ' is an allusion to Ecclesiastes 12:12 or a similar idiom. The Talmud citation '(יומא ד:)' refers to Yoma 4b. The Kli Yakar reads 'כַּחַמָּה' (lens) following the Rabbinic tradition in Leviticus Rabbah 34:1 and Sotah 58b contrasting Moses's face as a lens (מאירה) vs. a covering (אטומה); the text here reads 'כַּחַמָּה הַמְּאִירָה' which appears correct."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:45.254051473Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}